# RME invoice correction — pre-payment activation contract (rule 191)

Evidence: `docs/sprints/activation-rme-invoice-correction-prepayment-1.md`.
Extends rule 190; where they disagree about what EXISTS, this rule is newer.

## 1. Activation is four separate facts — never one (ALL labels)
1. **Code deployed ≠ routes registered ≠ flag ON ≠ feature verified.** The
   correction capability is operationally active only when ALL of: owner
   decisions D1, D7, D10, D21 recorded with authoritative evidence; the D21 price
   authority enforced; an operational role granted the three permissions
   through `RoleSeeder`; the flag ON through the environment + config cache
   rebuilt as the runtime user; and an authenticated maker/checker pilot proven.
2. Status at this release: **FEATURE_FLAG_OFF**, `ACTIVATION_SCOPE=NONE`. No GO
   tag named `activation-rme-invoice-correction-prepayment-1-go` may exist
   while the flag is OFF or any of the four decisions is PENDING.

## 2. Owner decisions — PENDING_OWNER_DECISION
3. D1 (pre-payment approval authority), D7 (receipt versioning policy), D10
   (does a PENDING correction block payment), D21 (price authority) are still
   **PENDING**. The audit register's "Rekomendasi" column and any brief's
   "proposed policies" are PROPOSALS, never approvals. Decision codes are per
   register — the KTP-OCR "D7 APPROVED" is a different decision.
4. Never self-approve a financial business decision. Never enable the flag, grant
   a correction permission to an operational role, or run a real correction until
   the owner decides — that is the fail-closed answer, not a blocker to route around.

## 3. Payment ↔ invoice version binding — IMPLEMENTED, ACTIVE_IN_PRODUCTION
5. Every payment path (`pay`, `allocateControlPayment`, `allocateVisitPayment`)
   must settle the invoice version the cashier SAW. The form carries
   `invoice_versions[<invoice id>] = <correction_version>` for EVERY invoice it
   displays (the paid invoice, control carry-over parents, selectable receivables),
   rendered from the CURRENT rows — never from `old()`.
6. **Enforced in the SERVICE, under the invoice row lock** (`assertFormVersion()`),
   for every invoice the batch would settle. A missing or non-whole version is
   `[INVOICE_VERSION_MISSING]`; a different version is `[INVOICE_VERSION_CHANGED]`.
   Programmatic callers are bound too — a request rule alone would leave them unbound.
7. The comparison MUST read the LOCKED row. Correction apply takes the same lock
   first, so: correction commits first ⇒ the stale form is refused; payment commits
   first ⇒ the correction goes STALE (rule 190 §8). Moving the check before the lock
   lets a correction commit in between — the PostgreSQL race test kills that mutant.
8. A conflict rolls the WHOLE batch back (no payment, no status change, no visit
   completion) and is audited `RME_PAYMENT_REFUSED_INVOICE_VERSION` AFTER the
   rollback (it records an attempt). Payload: ids, invoice number, branch, reason
   code, payment path, expected/current version, status — never the typed amount,
   a note or a reference number. `RmePaymentService::recordVersionRefusal()` is the
   ONLY writer; it must run at transaction level 0 and, if the audit write fails,
   logs (codes/ids only) and still lets the refusal happen — an audit outage never
   turns a refusal into a 500 or a payment.
9. The FormRequest repeats the comparison WITHOUT a lock, for every invoice the
   payment would settle (paid invoice + carry-over parents / selected receivables),
   BEFORE the amount cap — so a stale page reads "tagihan telah dikoreksi", never a
   misleading "amount exceeds the remaining balance". It audits its refusals
   through the same writer (that is where the commonest case is caught). It is
   never the authority.
9a. **Authorize before validation.** `CreateRmePaymentRequest::authorize()` runs the
   `pay` policy (branch scope) and `belongsToVisit` BEFORE any validation hook,
   because those hooks read an invoice's balance and version: running them for
   another branch's cashier answered "this invoice was corrected". The controller
   keeps both checks as a second layer.
9b. After a version refusal the re-rendered page restores neither the typed amount
   nor the receivable selection: the total changed, so the cashier re-enters them
   against the bill now shown. A reflexive resubmit can otherwise record a smaller
   amount than the new total as a partial payment.
10. **This is not D10.** A SUBMITTED/APPROVED correction changes nothing the cashier
    sees; a payment against it is still accepted and the correction then goes STALE.
    Whether a pending correction should block payment is D10, still PENDING.
11. No new lock: the version is read from rows the payment already locks, in the
    existing order (paid invoice first, then parents/receivables in summary order).
    Pre-existing residual, NOT introduced here: two concurrent batches for one
    patient that lock the same invoices in opposite orders (visit A collecting B
    while B collects A) can deadlock (PostgreSQL 40P01 → 500, nothing written).
12. Tests that are not about binding use `rmeFreshPaymentForm($invoice, $data)`
    (tests/Pest.php): the versions a page rendered NOW would carry. A stale-form test
    must build `invoice_versions` explicitly — the helper never overwrites one.

## 4. Price & discount authority (D21) — PENDING_OWNER_DECISION, BLOCKED
13. Cashiers still type item prices and discounts freely (audit BCF-01). Rp0 is
    still refused by the correction proposal (no zero line, no zero/negative total).
14. Measured 2026-10-17 (production, read-only): tariffs exist ONLY for TLK1
    (11 treatments); SPN4 — the only branch that has billed — has none, and all 7
    production invoice items are `NO_TARIFF`. A tariff-based price authority would
    therefore refuse every Sunu invoice today. D21 needs an owner decision AND
    tariff data for every billing branch before it can be enforced. Never invent a
    percentage limit, a default tariff, or a "same as TLK1" fallback.

## 5. Permissions — FEATURE_FLAG_OFF, NOT GRANTED
15. `request_rme_invoice_correction`, `approve_rme_invoice_correction`,
    `apply_rme_invoice_correction` are held ONLY by Super Admin (via `'*'`). No
    operational role (Kasir, Admin Klinik, Front Office, Supervisor RME) gets them
    before D1. A grant goes through `RoleSeeder` only — measure the production
    delta first (seed a scratch DB, diff `role_has_permissions`).
16. Maker ≠ checker stays a SERVICE rule under the row lock, per ACCOUNT — a user
    holding several roles still cannot approve their own request, and `Gate::before`
    means a policy can never be the guarantee.

## 6. Oversight, documents, scope — unchanged (rule 190)
17. Finance Klinik and Data Analyst stay READ-ONLY. Frozen v0/vN document versions,
    STALE-on-payment, one active correction per invoice, branch scope and the D23
    lab-candidate refusal are unchanged.
18. FUTURE_SCOPE, never reported active: post-payment correction, refund, reversal,
    patient credit, Rp0 adjustment, settlement, pending-correction payment block (D10).

## 7. Rollback & evidence
19. Rollback of this release is a code rollback only: no migration, no seeder, no
    flag. The binding refuses nothing a freshly rendered page submits; a page opened
    before the deploy is refused once with "muat ulang halaman" — never a data loss.
20. When activation is finally attempted it needs, in order: recorded decisions →
    D21 enforcement + tariffs per billing branch → RoleSeeder grant → flag via
    environment + config cache as `daengtisiams` → authenticated pilot with two
    distinct real accounts → financial reconciliation. A missing step is NO_GO.
